{"header":"<h1>Module <code><span>Format.Args</span></code></h1>","type":"documentation","uses_katex":false,"breadcrumbs":[{"name":"Index","href":"../../../../index.html","kind":"leaf-page"},{"name":"melange","href":"../../../index.html","kind":"page"},{"name":"Stdlib","href":"../../index.html","kind":"module"},{"name":"Format","href":"../index.html","kind":"module"},{"name":"Args","href":"#","kind":"module"}],"toc":[],"source_anchor":null,"preamble":"<p>The <code>Args</code> module defines a heterogeneous list type, which can be used as the argument of <code>printf</code>-like functions.</p><p>It is not required to open this module when using the functions that accept an <code>Args.t</code>. Thanks to type-based disambiguation, the type is inferred automatically, so the list syntax <code>[x; y; z]</code> can be used directly for heterogeneous lists.</p><p>An example:</p><pre class=\"language-ocaml\"><code>  (* without opening Args *)\u000A  Format.lprintf &quot;%s %d %.02f@.&quot; [ &quot;ocaml&quot;; 42; 3.14 ]\u000A\u000A  (* or with explicit construction *)\u000A  let lst = let open Format.Args in &quot;ocaml&quot; :: [ 42; 3.14 ] @ [ 'c' ] in\u000A  Format.lprintf &quot;%s %d %.02f %c@.&quot; lst</code></pre><ul class=\"at-tags\"><li class=\"since\"><span class=\"at-tag\">since</span> 5.5</li></ul>","content":"<div class=\"odoc-spec\"><div class=\"spec type anchored\" id=\"type-t\"><a href=\"#type-t\" class=\"anchor\"></a><code><span><span class=\"keyword\">type</span> <span>('a, 'r) t</span></span><span> = </span></code><ol><li id=\"type-t.[]\" class=\"def variant constructor anchored\"><a href=\"#type-t.[]\" class=\"anchor\"></a><code><span>| </span><span><span class=\"constructor\">[]</span> : <span><span>(<span class=\"type-var\">'r</span>, <span class=\"type-var\">'r</span>)</span> <a href=\"#type-t\">t</a></span></span></code></li><li id=\"type-t.::\" class=\"def variant constructor anchored\"><a href=\"#type-t.::\" class=\"anchor\"></a><code><span>| </span><span><span class=\"constructor\">::</span> : <span class=\"type-var\">'a</span> * <span><span>(<span class=\"type-var\">'b</span>, <span class=\"type-var\">'r</span>)</span> <a href=\"#type-t\">t</a></span> <span class=\"arrow\">&#45;&gt;</span> <span><span>(<span><span class=\"type-var\">'a</span> <span class=\"arrow\">&#45;&gt;</span></span> <span class=\"type-var\">'b</span>, <span class=\"type-var\">'r</span>)</span> <a href=\"#type-t\">t</a></span></span></code></li></ol></div></div><div class=\"odoc-spec\"><div class=\"spec value anchored\" id=\"val-apply\"><a href=\"#val-apply\" class=\"anchor\"></a><code><span><span class=\"keyword\">val</span> apply : <span><span class=\"type-var\">'a</span> <span class=\"arrow\">&#45;&gt;</span></span> <span><span><span>(<span class=\"type-var\">'a</span>, <span class=\"type-var\">'r</span>)</span> <a href=\"#type-t\">t</a></span> <span class=\"arrow\">&#45;&gt;</span></span> <span class=\"type-var\">'r</span></span></code></div></div><div class=\"odoc-spec\"><div class=\"spec value anchored\" id=\"val-(@)\"><a href=\"#val-(@)\" class=\"anchor\"></a><code><span><span class=\"keyword\">val</span> (@) : <span><span><span>(<span class=\"type-var\">'a</span>, <span class=\"type-var\">'r1</span>)</span> <a href=\"#type-t\">t</a></span> <span class=\"arrow\">&#45;&gt;</span></span> <span><span><span>(<span class=\"type-var\">'r1</span>, <span class=\"type-var\">'r2</span>)</span> <a href=\"#type-t\">t</a></span> <span class=\"arrow\">&#45;&gt;</span></span> <span><span>(<span class=\"type-var\">'a</span>, <span class=\"type-var\">'r2</span>)</span> <a href=\"#type-t\">t</a></span></span></code></div></div>"}